# Vectis Target Adapter - Crux Cross-Platform Development

## Overview

The Vectis target adapter contributes its `shape` / `build` / `merge` briefs to the current Specify workflow. The adapter generates cross-platform applications using the Crux framework (Rust), SwiftUI iOS shells, and Kotlin/Jetpack Compose Android shells from synthesised Specify artifacts (`spec.md` + `design.md`) plus operator-curated `tokens.yaml` / `assets.yaml` build inputs.

**Purpose**: Carry the Vectis `shape` / `build` / `merge` briefs and the references the briefs lift from, plus a `screenshots`-driven layout inferer that emits spatial Evidence for the matching source adapter.

## Adapter layout

```text
/adapters/targets/vectis/
├── adapter.yaml                     # Target adapter manifest
├── briefs/                          # shape, build (with sub-briefs), merge
├── codex/                           # Vectis rules
├── examples/                        # Operator-facing example artifacts
├── references/                      # Crux idiom prose, design-system docs, layout-inferer contract
├── rules/
│   └── vectis.mdc                   # This file
└── schemas/                         # README pointing to canonical tool-owned schemas
```

The adapter manifest, the three briefs (`shape`, `build`, `merge`), the WASI tool declaration, the Vectis codex, and the operator-facing `tokens.yaml` example all live under [`adapters/targets/vectis/`](..); runtime schemas ([`composition.schema.json`](https://schemas.specify.dev/vectis/composition.schema.json), [`tokens.schema.json`](https://schemas.specify.dev/vectis/tokens.schema.json), [`assets.schema.json`](https://schemas.specify.dev/vectis/assets.schema.json)) are tool-owned in the CLI repo and retrievable via `specify tool schema vectis <name>`.

The Vectis writer / reviewer / template-updater behavior (`core-writer`, `core-reviewer`, `test-writer`, `ios-writer`, `ios-reviewer`, `android-writer`, `android-reviewer`, `template-updater`) lives inline in [`../briefs/build.md`](../briefs/build.md) (writers and reviewers) and [`../briefs/merge.md`](../briefs/merge.md) (post-merge host cap-matrix re-verification). The `vectis:image-layout-inferer` behavior lives in the [`screenshots` source adapter](../../../sources/screenshots/adapter.yaml).

## Common Patterns

### New Crux Application

A Crux app is defined as a single feature with platforms declared in the proposal. A feature like `todo-app` with platforms `core, ios, android` produces one spec covering all behavioural requirements (core logic plus platform-specific sections), one design document, and one task list. The build brief reads `spec.md` + `design.md`, regenerates `composition.yaml` from the upstream spatial claims that core synthesis folded in, and then drives core, iOS, and Android generation in that order.

### Feature Update

Updated feature specs and design generate targeted changes across all affected platforms: modified Event variants and `update()` logic in the core, updated screen views in iOS and Android, and regenerated tokens if the design system changed. All changes flow from a single feature spec.

### Adding a Platform

When a new platform is added, the feature spec gains a new platform section (e.g., `## Android Shell Requirements`), the design gains a platform details section, and the build brief invokes the new platform's writer pass. Existing specs and code are unaffected.

### Design System Update

Token changes in `tokens.yaml` are picked up by each shell writer, which emits shell-local theme code under `iOS/<App>/Theme/` (iOS) and `Android/.../ui/theme/` (Android). Token changes can be part of a feature change or a standalone feature; `tokens.yaml` and `assets.yaml` remain operator-curated and are consumed by the build brief, never synthesised.

## Troubleshooting

### Generated Core Incomplete

- Are specs complete with requirements and scenarios?
- Does `design.md` include the full Crux type system (Model, Event, ViewModel, Effect)?
- Are adapters explicitly listed in the design?

### iOS Shell Build Fails

- Does the core exist with `shared/src/app.rs`?
- Run the explicit iOS host checks from the shell directory: `make typegen`, `make package`, and `make xcode`.
- If those pass but the iOS app panics with "UniFFI contract version mismatch", the installed cargo-swift version is incompatible with the active Vectis version pins. Reinstall a compatible cargo-swift release and rerun the host checks.

### Android Shell Build Fails

- Does the core exist with `shared/src/app.rs`?
- Was `make build` run to generate Kotlin types and UniFFI bindings?
- Is `org.gradle.java.home` set to Java 21 in `gradle.properties`?
- Does the `Application` class set `System.setProperty("uniffi.component.shared.libraryOverride", "shared")` before any UniFFI class is loaded?
- Are Rust Android targets installed? (`rustup target list --installed | grep android`)

### Tests Don't Match Expected Behavior

- Are spec scenarios accurate?
- Were requirements changed after generation?

